# PERIODIZATION

Version 1.0

---

# Purpose

The Periodization model describes the scientific principles governing the long-term organization of training.

It explains how training stress should be distributed over time to maximize adaptation while minimizing unnecessary fatigue and injury risk.

The Periodization model provides scientific knowledge.

It does not prescribe training plans.

That responsibility belongs to the Periodization Engine.

---

# Definition

Periodization is the planned organization of training variables over time.

Its objective is to maximize long-term performance by manipulating:

Volume

Intensity

Frequency

Exercise Selection

Recovery

Specificity

Training Stress

---

# Philosophy

Performance is not built by individual sessions.

Performance emerges from the accumulation of appropriately sequenced training stimuli.

Every session should contribute to a broader objective.

---

# Fundamental Principles

Effective periodization respects several principles.

Specificity

Progressive Overload

Variation

Recovery

Individualization

Long-Term Development

These principles should remain compatible.

None exists independently.

---

# Training Variables

Periodization manipulates multiple variables.

Training Volume

Training Intensity

Training Density

Frequency

Exercise Complexity

Movement Velocity

Rest Duration

Technical Demand

Specificity

No variable should dominate indefinitely.

---

# Time Scales

Training is organized across several time horizons.

Session

↓

Microcycle

↓

Mesocycle

↓

Macrocycle

↓

Long-Term Athlete Development

Each level influences the others.

---

# Session

The smallest planning unit.

Objective

Deliver a precise training stimulus.

---

# Microcycle

Typically one week.

Objectives

Organize fatigue.

Balance physical qualities.

Coordinate recovery.

---

# Mesocycle

Typically four to eight weeks.

Objective

Develop one dominant adaptation.

Examples

Maximum Strength

Power

Hypertrophy

Aerobic Development

Competition Preparation

---

# Macrocycle

Several months.

Objective

Prepare the athlete for major performance goals.

Examples

Competition Season

Annual Planning

Long-Term Development

---

# Long-Term Athlete Development

Performance should improve across years.

Not merely across weeks.

Long-term planning prioritizes sustainable progression.

---

# Adaptation Sequencing

Different adaptations should be developed in logical order.

Examples

General Capacity

↓

Maximum Strength

↓

Power

↓

Sport-Specific Power

↓

Competition Readiness

The sequence depends on the athlete.

---

# Variation

Variation prevents stagnation.

Variation does not imply randomness.

Every change should have a purpose.

Examples

Exercise Variation

Volume Variation

Intensity Variation

Movement Variation

Recovery Variation

---

# Progressive Overload

Training stress gradually increases over time.

Progression may involve:

Load

Volume

Complexity

Density

Velocity

Specificity

Progressive overload should remain conservative.

---

# Deload

Deload is an integral part of periodization.

Objectives

Reduce accumulated fatigue.

Preserve adaptations.

Prepare future progression.

Deload is planned,

not reactive.

---

# Residual Training Effects

Different physical qualities persist for different durations.

Examples

Maximum Strength

↓

Moderate persistence.

Power

↓

Short persistence.

Aerobic Capacity

↓

Long persistence.

Periodization should account for these differences.

---

# Concurrent Training

Multiple qualities may develop simultaneously.

Potential interference exists.

Examples

Maximum Strength

+

Power

↓

Generally compatible.

Maximum Hypertrophy

+

High-Volume Endurance

↓

Potential interference.

Planning should minimize conflicting adaptations.

---

# Individualization

Optimal periodization depends on:

Training Age

Sport

Competition Calendar

Recovery Capacity

Training Availability

Injury History

Lifestyle

No universal model exists.

---

# Combat Sports

Combat sports require multiple qualities simultaneously.

Examples

Strength

Power

Explosiveness

Speed

Grip

Technical Skill

Energy System Development

Psychological Readiness

Periodization must balance these competing demands.

---

# Competition Preparation

As competition approaches,

training gradually shifts toward:

Higher Specificity

Reduced Volume

Maintained Intensity

Greater Technical Precision

Improved Recovery

Freshness becomes increasingly important.

---

# Transition Phase

Following competition,

training emphasizes:

Recovery

Regeneration

General Physical Preparation

Psychological Recovery

Preparation for the next cycle.

---

# Monitoring

Periodization requires continuous evaluation.

Possible indicators include:

Performance

Recovery

Fatigue

Training Compliance

Pain

Competition Results

Readiness

Monitoring informs future planning.

---

